:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>false</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:Key>S160fc2117-4f2f-4797-a79b-cd2329fc5a6c711cc6d4-936d-4401-80f8-cd226e0b85f96260a99f-7361-44eb-9ad4-805c568f6bef</a:Key><a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:ChangedBy i:nil="true"/><b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>f9cfa17b-9098-466d-94a6-c2c5c8cfa594</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid><b:FlowGuid>711cc6d4-936d-4401-80f8-cd226e0b85f9</b:FlowGuid><b:Id>260</b:Id><b:InteractionKey>60fc2117-4f2f-4797-a79b-cd2329fc5a6c:711cc6d4-936d-4401-80f8-cd226e0b85f9:6260a99f-7361-44eb-9ad4-805c568f6bef</b:InteractionKey><b:InteractionString i:nil="true"/><b:ModifiedAt>0001-01-01T00:00:00</b:ModifiedAt><b:Priority>High</b:Priority><b:Properties><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Title</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing the 2.5 Update Delivery Status (Driver)  Process</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing is when a process or entity is something other than its claimed identity. Examples include substituting a process, a file, website or a network address.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key><a:Value>2.5 Update Delivery Status (Driver)  may be spoofed by an attacker and this may lead to unauthorized access to shipments.dat . Consider using a standard authentication mechanism to identify the source process.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>Delivery Status</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>High</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>60fc2117-4f2f-4797-a79b-cd2329fc5a6c</b:SourceGuid><b:State>AutoGenerated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>6260a99f-7361-44eb-9ad4-805c568f6bef</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>S1</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>false</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:Key>S7.160fc2117-4f2f-4797-a79b-cd2329fc5a6c711cc6d4-936d-4401-80f8-cd226e0b85f96260a99f-7361-44eb-9ad4-805c568f6bef</a:Key><a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:ChangedBy i:nil="true"/><b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>f9cfa17b-9098-466d-94a6-c2c5c8cfa594</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid><b:FlowGuid>711cc6d4-936d-4401-80f8-cd226e0b85f9</b:FlowGuid><b:Id>261</b:Id><b:InteractionKey>60fc2117-4f2f-4797-a79b-cd2329fc5a6c:711cc6d4-936d-4401-80f8-cd226e0b85f9:6260a99f-7361-44eb-9ad4-805c568f6bef</b:InteractionKey><b:InteractionString i:nil="true"/><b:ModifiedAt>0001-01-01T00:00:00</b:ModifiedAt><b:Priority>High</b:Priority><b:Properties><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Title</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing of Destination Data Store shipments.dat </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing is when a process or entity is something other than its claimed identity. Examples include substituting a process, a file, website or a network address.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key><a:Value>shipments.dat  may be spoofed by an attacker and this may lead to data being written to the attacker's target instead of shipments.dat . Consider using a standard authentication mechanism to identify the destination data store.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>Delivery Status</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>High</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>60fc2117-4f2f-4797-a79b-cd2329fc5a6c</b:SourceGuid><b:State>AutoGenerated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>6260a99f-7361-44eb-9ad4-805c568f6bef</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>S7.1</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>false</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:Key>T1860fc2117-4f2f-4797-a79b-cd2329fc5a6c711cc6d4-936d-4401-80f8-cd226e0b85f96260a99f-7361-44eb-9ad4-805c568f6bef</a:Key><a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:ChangedBy>PC\ahmad</b:ChangedBy><b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>f9cfa17b-9098-466d-94a6-c2c5c8cfa594</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid><b:FlowGuid>711cc6d4-936d-4401-80f8-cd226e0b85f9</b:FlowGuid><b:Id>262</b:Id><b:InteractionKey>60fc2117-4f2f-4797-a79b-cd2329fc5a6c:711cc6d4-936d-4401-80f8-cd226e0b85f9:6260a99f-7361-44eb-9ad4-805c568f6bef</b:InteractionKey><b:InteractionString i:nil="true"/><b:ModifiedAt>2026-06-06T17:49:50.9691298+03:00</b:ModifiedAt><b:Priority>Low</b:Priority><b:Properties><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Title</a:Key><a:Value>The shipments.dat  Data Store Could Be Corrupted</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key><a:Value>Tampering</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Tampering is the act of altering the bits. Tampering with a process involves changing bits in the running process. Similarly, Tampering with a data flow involves changing bits on the wire or between two running processes.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Data flowing across Delivery Status may be tampered with by an attacker. This may lead to corruption of shipments.dat . </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>StateInformation</a:Key><a:Value>Mitigated by SEC-DATA-01 + state-machine integrity: ShipmentStatus.canMoveTo() (called by Shipment.advanceTo()) rejects illegal transitions. Only valid status values (PENDING, PICKED_UP, IN_TRANSIT, DELIVERED) can be written, parsed via ShipmentStatus.fromString() which throws on invalid input.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>Delivery Status</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>Low</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>60fc2117-4f2f-4797-a79b-cd2329fc5a6c</b:SourceGuid><b:State>Mitigated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>6260a99f-7361-44eb-9ad4-805c568f6bef</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>T18</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>false</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:Key>R860fc2117-4f2f-4797-a79b-cd2329fc5a6c711cc6d4-936d-4401-80f8-cd226e0b85f96260a99f-7361-44eb-9ad4-805c568f6bef</a:Key><a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:ChangedBy i:nil="true"/><b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>f9cfa17b-9098-466d-94a6-c2c5c8cfa594</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid><b:FlowGuid>711cc6d4-936d-4401-80f8-cd226e0b85f9</b:FlowGuid><b:Id>263</b:Id><b:InteractionKey>60fc2117-4f2f-4797-a79b-cd2329fc5a6c:711cc6d4-936d-4401-80f8-cd226e0b85f9:6260a99f-7361-44eb-9ad4-805c568f6bef</b:InteractionKey><b:InteractionString i:nil="true"/><b:ModifiedAt>0001-01-01T00:00:00</b:ModifiedAt><b:Priority>High</b:Priority><b:Properties><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Title</a:Key><a:Value>Data Store Denies shipments.dat  Potentially Writing Data</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key><a:Value>Repudiation</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Repudiation threats involve an adversary denying that something happened.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key><a:Value>shipments.dat  claims that it did not write data received from an entity on the other side of the trust boundary. Consider using logging or auditing to record the source, time, and summary of the received data.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>Delivery Status</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>High</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>60fc2117-4f2f-4797-a79b-cd2329fc5a6c</b:SourceGuid><b:State>AutoGenerated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>6260a99f-7361-44eb-9ad4-805c568f6bef</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>R8</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>false</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:Key>D260fc2117-4f2f-4797-a79b-cd2329fc5a6c711cc6d4-936d-4401-80f8-cd226e0b85f96260a99f-7361-44eb-9ad4-805c568f6bef</a:Key><a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:ChangedBy i:nil="true"/><b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>f9cfa17b-9098-466d-94a6-c2c5c8cfa594</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid><b:FlowGuid>711cc6d4-936d-4401-80f8-cd226e0b85f9</b:FlowGuid><b:Id>264</b:Id><b:InteractionKey>60fc2117-4f2f-4797-a79b-cd2329fc5a6c:711cc6d4-936d-4401-80f8-cd226e0b85f9:6260a99f-7361-44eb-9ad4-805c568f6bef</b:InteractionKey><b:InteractionString i:nil="true"/><b:ModifiedAt>0001-01-01T00:00:00</b:ModifiedAt><b:Priority>High</b:Priority><b:Properties><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Title</a:Key><a:Value>Potential Excessive Resource Consumption for 2.5 Update Delivery Status (Driver)  or shipments.dat </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key><a:Value>Denial Of Service</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Denial of Service happens when the process or a datastore is not able to service incoming requests or perform up to spec.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Does 2.5 Update Delivery Status (Driver)  or shipments.dat  take explicit steps to control resource consumption? Resource consumption attacks can be hard to deal with, and there are times that it makes sense to let the OS do the job. Be careful that your resource requests don't deadlock, and that they do timeout.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>Delivery Status</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>High</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>60fc2117-4f2f-4797-a79b-cd2329fc5a6c</b:SourceGuid><b:State>AutoGenerated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>6260a99f-7361-44eb-9ad4-805c568f6bef</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>D2</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>false</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:Key>D460fc2117-4f2f-4797-a79b-cd2329fc5a6c711